# ============================================================
# ORCHESTRATOR NOTEBOOK  (time-boxed + resumable)  -- v6
#
# v6 = v5 + faster scheduling. Same sync, same results, same logs.
#
#   L. TWO PIPELINES AT A TIME BY DEFAULT (the "Run it" cell calls
#      run_orchestrator(pipeline_configs, max_parallel=2)). The run of
#      2026-11-10 was ~4h of work done one after the other (MEDS 86 min,
#      NOTES_C 51 min, ...); most pipelines do not depend on each other, so
#      two run side by side. Dependencies are kept exactly as in v4/v5: a
#      pipeline starts only after every earlier pipeline it is tied to has
#      finished, so it reads the same inputs as in a sequential run.
#      max_parallel=1 = the v5 sequential loop.
#   M. LONGEST CHAIN FIRST (parallel mode only). Of the pipelines that are
#      ready, the one with the longest expected chain (its own expected time
#      plus the longest chain of pipelines waiting for it) starts first:
#      TBL_PAT_ID and PAT_ENC, then MEDS with the light pipelines next to it
#      (NOTES_C after MEDS, header P), so the long ones do not end up last
#      and alone. A pipeline that no longer fits the time box is
#      skipped and runs in the next round; shorter ones may still start.
#      Sequential mode keeps config order and v5's stop rule.
#   N. While pipelines share the session, a pipeline can wait for free cores
#      (Spark FIFO scheduling), so it can take much longer than alone. In
#      parallel mode the OVERRUN stop (header I) therefore uses a bigger
#      margin: max(2 x expected, expected + 90 min)
#      (overrun_min_minutes_parallel). It still catches a pipeline that hangs
#      with Spark jobs running (like NOTES_C on 2026-11-09) before the 4h
#      kill. The STALL stop (no Spark job at all for stall_minutes) is
#      unchanged, and after any parallel stop the next round runs
#      sequentially (header G), as before.
#   O. The start banner prints the Spark scheduler mode (FIFO / FAIR).
#   P. TWO HEAVY PIPELINES NEVER RUN TOGETHER (parallel mode). A pipeline
#      whose recent time (history, before the safety factors) is at least
#      heavy_minutes (30) is heavy: today MEDS and NOTES_C. A heavy pipeline
#      runs next to light ones, never next to another heavy one, so the two
#      biggest caches never compete for executor memory (a local test showed
#      that memory pressure can end in an OutOfMemoryError). A pipeline with
#      no history counts as heavy. heavy_minutes=None turns this off.
#
# ---- v5 notes ----
#
# v5 = v4 + two fixes from the 2026-11-09 run (7h36m wall clock for about
# 3.5h of pipeline work: TBL_ALLERGY_NOTES_C ran 2h50m in an older session
# until the 4h kill, and took 49 min in a fresh one). Same order, same
# results, same logs.
#
#   I. WATCHDOG IN SEQUENTIAL MODE TOO (watchdog=True, default). Each
#      pipeline runs in a worker thread and the notebook thread watches it.
#      The round stops cleanly (STATUS 'STOPPED_TIME_LIMIT', RUN_NOTE
#      'STALL: <name>' or 'OVERRUN: <name>') when the running pipeline
#        - has had no Spark job for stall_minutes (20), or
#        - its attempt runs longer than
#          max(overrun_factor (2) x expected, expected + overrun_min_minutes (30)).
#          Only for a pipeline with run history, and never for the first
#          pipeline of a fresh session (a new session cannot do better).
#      Its Spark jobs are cancelled, a thread dump is printed, and the next
#      round of the Until loop (fresh session) starts with that pipeline. A
#      pipeline stopped by the watchdog runs WITHOUT the watchdog for the
#      rest of that resume chain, so a genuinely slow day cannot loop.
#      watchdog=False (and max_parallel=1) = exactly the v4 sequential loop.
#   J. EXPECTED TIME from the last duration_history (3, was 5) successful
#      runs. A session-killed batch counts only if the pipeline has not
#      succeeded since (a kill caused by a hang no longer blocks it for the
#      next 5 runs).
#   K. A storage "server busy" error (503 ServerBusy / 429, e.g. the MEDS
#      failure of 2026-11-09) waits busy_retry_wait_seconds (180) before the
#      retry instead of 15 s.
#
# ---- v4 notes ----
#
# v4 = v3 + speed options. With the defaults (max_parallel=1) it runs
# exactly like v3: same order, same time box, same resume, same logs.
#
#   F. OPTIONAL PARALLEL (max_parallel=2..4). Pipelines that do not depend on
#      each other run at the same time in this Spark session. A pipeline
#      starts only after every earlier pipeline it is tied to has finished
#      (reads its target, same target, or cfg["depends_on"] -- the same rule
#      resume uses), so each one reads the same inputs as in a sequential run.
#      The time box applies to every start (expected time x
#      parallel_duration_factor, since pipelines running together are slower).
#      Log / batch table writes are serialized (one at a time).
#   G. HANG WATCHDOG (parallel mode). The earlier parallel try hung with 0
#      active Spark stages. If pipelines are running but no Spark job has run
#      for stall_minutes (20), the round prints a full thread dump (Python +
#      JVM + JVM deadlock check), stops cleanly with STATUS
#      'STOPPED_TIME_LIMIT' and RUN_NOTE 'PARALLEL_STALL: ...', and the next
#      round of the Until loop continues SEQUENTIALLY (max_parallel=1) from
#      where it stopped. Send the thread dump for the root cause.
#   H. Run-row updates use a Delta UPDATE on RUN_ID (no MERGE join).
#
# ---- v3 notes ----
#
# Requires MERGE_NB (%run it first) -- it uses
# run_batch_tracked_merge() and configure_spark_for_efficiency().
#
# Same as the previous (sequential) orchestrator, plus a fix for
# JOB_REACHED_MAX_LIFETIME ("Livy session ... cancelled because of
# reaching configured 4 hours lifetime"). The sync itself
# (run_batch_tracked_merge / deltaUpsertAndSync) and the pipeline order
# are unchanged; the log tables only get new columns.
#
#   A. TIME BOX. The clock is the Spark session's age. Before starting a
#      pipeline (or a retry) the orchestrator checks:
#        1. elapsed < start_cutoff_hours (default 3): after 3 hours no NEW
#           pipeline starts. The one already running keeps going and
#           finishes in the 3h-4h window.
#        2. elapsed + expected time must end before
#           session_limit_hours (4) - safety_margin_minutes (10).
#           expected time = the longest of the pipeline's recent runs
#           (SUCCESS attempts in ALLERGY_ETL_STEP_LOG, SUCCESS batches in
#           the batch table, and how long it had run when a session was
#           killed under it) x duration_safety_factor (1.25).
#           Never succeeded yet -> unknown_duration_minutes (90).
#      The first pipeline of a run in a fresh session (up for less than
#      fresh_session_minutes, 30) always starts: a new session cannot do
#      better. If a check fails the run STOPS CLEANLY before that
#      pipeline: nothing is left RUNNING, the run row gets
#      STATUS = 'STOPPED_TIME_LIMIT' and the pipelines not yet run go in
#      PENDING_PIPELINE_NAMES. Pipelines are never reordered or skipped
#      past (a later pipeline may read an earlier one's target).
#
#   B. RESUME (resume=True, default). If the previous run ended a short
#      while ago (resume_gap_minutes, default 60) without a full SUCCESS --
#      stopped at the time limit, killed with its session, or a pipeline
#      failed -- the new run skips the pipelines that already SUCCEEDED in
#      that chain of runs and does only the rest. A skipped pipeline that
#      depends on one that runs again (reads its target, same target, or
#      cfg["depends_on"]) runs again too, after it. A full SUCCESS ends the
#      chain, and so does a long gap: the next scheduled run does
#      everything.
#
#   C. AUTO CLEANUP of killed sessions (stale_after_hours, default =
#      session_limit_hours; None turns it off).
#        - ALLERGY_ETL_RUN_LOG: a RUNNING row whose Spark session started
#          more than session_limit_hours ago cannot be alive -> ABANDONED.
#          (Rows from before SESSION_START_DTTM existed use RUN_START_DTTM.)
#        - <batch_table>: the orchestrator tags its batches
#          (CREATED_BY = 'Fabric Notebook | ORCH_RUN_ID=<n>'). A RUNNING
#          batch whose run <n> is no longer RUNNING is dead -> FAILED, with
#          BATCH_END_DTTM = when that session died. Untagged RUNNING rows
#          (older runs, manual calls) are closed only once they are older
#          than stale_after_hours, as before. A live batch is never closed.
#
#   D. A time-limit stop does NOT raise (it is planned, the next round
#      resumes). A pipeline that failed in a round that stopped at the time
#      limit is retried first in the next round. Real failures raise at
#      the end of the last round (raise_on_failure=True), as before. A round
#      that would stop for time without finishing any new pipeline (its
#      first pipeline failed) raises instead, so the loop cannot repeat the
#      same round forever.
#
#      A round in an OLD session (Fabric reused an earlier round's session)
#      in which not even the first pipeline fits ends at once with
#      STOPPED_TIME_LIMIT; the old session dies at its lifetime limit at the
#      latest, and a later round gets a fresh session.
#
#   E. AUTO-CONTINUE. The last cell exits the notebook with the run STATUS
#      (notebookutils.notebook.exit), so a Data pipeline "Until" loop around
#      this notebook starts a fresh session while the exit value is
#      'STOPPED_TIME_LIMIT'.
# ============================================================

import re
import sys
import time
import threading
import traceback
from datetime import datetime, timedelta
from delta.tables import DeltaTable
from pyspark.sql.types import (
    StructType, StructField, LongType, StringType,
    TimestampType, IntegerType, DoubleType,
